## Visual judgement (metrics never override this)

I read both shipped PNGs (and the v2 before/after copies in temp) with the image-read tool.
**v2 honest verdict: FAIL on visual fidelity** — identical rectangular stamps on a 12.8 px lattice,
hard-edged isolated rock, flat khaki. That defect is real and the coordinator's read is accurate.
**v3 honest verdict: PASS on the reported visual defects, not yet a donor-perfect match.** The
lattice is gone: patches are now irregular, clustered, variable-size organic shapes with hairline cracks
and empty ground between them; the rock has a softened edge and a visible contact shadow, so it sits
on the ground; the whole tile has a gentle low-frequency tonal grade instead of a uniform fill. The
palette remains donor-family olive-khaki with R>G>B everywhere and no green cast.
**Remaining visual gap:** the reference panel is warm orange-tan (and is actually wooden siding); the
port is still noticeably cooler/darker and the dark patches read more "stained/eroded" than the
polygonal cracked-earth facets of donor `desert2/3`. Closing that gap would require tuning toward a
reference that the spec itself labels LOW confidence and that C1 explicitly rejects — so it is left
as an open question, not a silent palette change.

